WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.590
Film Courage: This is a tricky question but
it’s up to you if you want to answer it,

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.800
navigating Hollywood agendas.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.999
Sometimes what happens (and I clearly don’t
have to tell you this.

00:00:09.999 --> 00:00:15.780
I’m sure this has happened to you in your
career) but something will happen with someone,

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.840
it won’t even be “burning somebody,”
but they are offended.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.830
And then they come back to you years later
as if nothing had ever happened.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.150
Like you’re great friends or they “friend”
you on social media.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.239
As a professional, how do you handle that?

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:33.239
Do you say “You know what?

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:34.989
It’s water under the bridge.”

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:36.540
Are there levels?

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.570
Some it’s water under the bridge.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.160
Others it’s “Please stay over there [away
from me] I think I’m good.”

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.170
Others it’s “You know what?

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.350
It was a misunderstanding.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.350
I’m fine.”

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:54.170
Bill Duke, Actor, Director, Author, Producer:
I think it’s both.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:59.010
Some people you can forgive and forget.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.260
Some people you cannot forgive or forget.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:10.909
There are other people you can forgive and
not forget.

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:16.390
So there’s people you can forgive and forget.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.049
Forgive and not forget.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:20.270
It depends on what they’ve done.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:28.049
It if’s the kind of betrayal of a friend,
but they not only...you in the back,

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:36.680
they held the object and twisted it a few times
to make sure that whatever trust you had in

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.500
them was gone.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:55.140
It’s not that you wouldn’t work with them
again, it’s called trust but verify.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:07.800
I had a martial arts teacher once give us
lessons in terms of…the teacher said you

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:21.870
know “If a man wants to fight you, you run
away from him as hard and fast as you can.”

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:34.090
And he said “But if he runs after you, and
he catches you, you make sure he never chases

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.020
you again.”

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:45.520
He said “Revenge is a dish best served cold…”

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:55.360
Don’t shout, don’t scream, it’s like
playing chess.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.840
He said “Don’t be playin’ checkers in
a chess game.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.350
Play chess.”

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:11.500
It’s not about the outcome, it’s the process,
too.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.580
So betrayal happens all the time.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:21.270
Betrayal, rejection and this is part of our
industry, it’s that you…for the young

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:28.250
people coming up in this industry (Ben and
I talk about this all the time) they come

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:38.540
in with dreams and hopes nice chests and
behinds, faces, hair and bodies and muscles

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.450
and all that stuff.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.830
And we were talking yesterday because Ben
is going into the acting business, right?

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:46.830
[gestures to someone off-camera].

00:03:46.830 --> 00:04:00.680
So I said “Look up the percentage of AFTRA
and SAG actors in the Union (the percentage)

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:09.120
that work annually out of 100%” Guess what
the number was?

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.490
20.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:19.310
And out of the 20% how many made over $50,000
a year?

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:25.440
[Speaks to someone off-camera “A $100,000
a year”]

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.950
About 10% make over $100,000 a year.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.590
Just deal with those numbers.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:38.910
You are coming in here with hope and dreams,
right?

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:47.980
Along with thousands of other people who look
as good as you on the same day you’re coming.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:56.350
They say don’t take rejection personally
but…[exhales].

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:02.450
If you get rejected once or twice, you can
understand that.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:08.990
But suppose you’re rejected every other
day for 9 years?

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:14.960
What does that do to you?

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.930
It creates something called pain.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:20.930
Self-deprecation.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.340
Self-doubt.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:29.850
And a lot of people bury those things with
liquor, drugs and I did that for awhile when

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.460
I first started because it was just so painful.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.260
Thank God I found meditation.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:41.960
But I see kids out here now in the street
and they came here with hopes and dreams and

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:47.490
they’re…and when I say literally they
are on the street.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.060
It’s sad.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.630
There’s hope.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:56.010
I mean, there’s luck and hope, everybody
should keep dreaming.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:58.100
But they should understand the business, too.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:02.670
And the good part about today is you don’t
have to wait to be discovered.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:06.150
You can put podcasts and webisodes up.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:14.080
If it’s something relevant and you get 1
or 2 million eyeballs guess what?

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.960
They come to you.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:23.140
It’s Always Sunny in Philadelphia, it’s
been out for 10 years now, right?

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:26.310
It started out as webisodes.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:30.110
Issa Rae, Awkward Black Girls, right?

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.010
Millions of eyeballs.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:36.830
Has a deal with HBO.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:45.200
TANGERINE at Sundance 2 years ago, a movie
made with 4 iPhones.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.180
Nobodies waiting.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:53.240
“I’m waiting for them to see how wonderful
I am.”

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.090
These guys got tired of waiting, man.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.150
They said “I’m going to discover myself.”

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.710
And they did and guess what?

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:05.480
When they’ve got that kind of inner confidence
and self-evaluation, something changed, something

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:08.580
happened.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:09.080
And good for them.

